      "S4",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 53,
    "html": "<p>Ralph Parsons was an American engineer who founded a major international construction and engineering company in Los Angeles. The son of a Long Island fisherman, he trained in machine design and served in the Navy before entering industry. His company built refineries, industrial plants and infrastructure, creating the fortune behind his charitable foundation.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Winning engineering work through relationships became a deliberate strategy for Ralph Parsons. With executive Milton Lewis, he decided to seek negotiated contracts rather than compete chiefly through bids. The company's officers cultivated clients over years, aiming to demonstrate a personal understanding of their needs. Parsons also placed considerable weight on recruiting and keeping capable engineers.\n\nPhotography offered him another outlet beyond industrial projects. His pictures became part of the offices of the foundation he established, which began as his company's charitable arm. He left it both company shares and cash, providing resources for grantmaking that continued after his death. The foundation later became financially independent of the business.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Full court factual section establishes the negotiated-contract strategy jointly with Lewis. Primary foundation biography supports photography, bequest and subsequent independence; corporate anniversary account corroborates recruitment emphasis."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 108,
    "html": "<p>Winning engineering work through relationships became a deliberate strategy for Ralph Parsons. With executive Milton Lewis, he decided to seek negotiated contracts rather than compete chiefly through bids. The company's officers cultivated clients over years, aiming to demonstrate a personal understanding of their needs. Parsons also placed considerable weight on recruiting and keeping capable engineers.</p>\r\n<p>Photography offered him another outlet beyond industrial projects. His pictures became part of the offices of the foundation he established, which began as his company's charitable arm. He left it both company shares and cash, providing resources for grantmaking that continued after his death. The foundation later became financially independent of the business.</p>\r\n"
  },
